course: dictionary
lesson: 6584
title: Nipple: one word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. Read the words around it. «She has no nipples, and the babies lap milk from patches of skin.» The sentence is about babies drinking milk from a mother's body, so a nipple here is a body part.
explain: Ask yourself what word would fit. In «The echidna has no nipples, so its babies get milk from patches of skin», the clues are babies, milk and skin. A nipple must be the part of a body where milk comes out. Then swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: The same word can mean something very different in another place. A baby's bottle has a nipple too, and a machine can have a small nipple called a nozzle. Look at what the sentence is about, and the clues will tell you which meaning is right.
fact: A nipple is a small raised point in the middle of each breast, or on a man's or boy's chest.
fact: All mammals feed their young with milk. Mothers feed babies milk through their nipples.
fact: The platypus and the echidna lay eggs, and they have no nipples. Their babies get milk from patches of skin.
fact: In the US, a nipple can mean the rubber or plastic teat on a baby's bottle. In the UK, this is called a teat.
fact: A grease nipple is a small nozzle on a machine, where grease can be pushed in.
exercise: «The echidna has no nipples, so its babies get milk from patches of skin.» What is a nipple in this sentence? | A kind of egg | A machine part for pumping oil | A type of spiky back | *A small raised point where milk comes out of the body
exercise: «The baby sucked milk from the rubber nipple on its bottle.» In this sentence, what is a nipple? | *A rubber teat on a baby's bottle | A place on a chest | A soft blanket | A small hard shell
exercise: «The mother platypus lays small leathery eggs in a burrow.» Find and copy one word that tells you the platypus is an animal that lays eggs. | riverbank | *eggs | burrow | mother
test: In the UK, what is the rubber part on a baby's bottle called? | A nozzle on a pipe | *A teat | A spike | A grease nipple
test: In «grease nipple», what does nipple mean? | A raised point on a chest | A baby's teat | *A small nozzle on a machine | A kind of egg
exam: Mia reads, «All mammals feed their young with milk, but the platypus has no nipples.» Which clue tells her that nipple means a body part? | *The idea of mammals feeding their young with milk | The word milk and the idea of feeding young | The word platypus and its name | The word has in the sentence
